title: A 4-vCPU, 8 GB machine costs €30 a month at Hetzner, $48 at DigitalOcean, and €7.50 at Contabo if you commit to two years; EC2's nearest costs $59, or $41 reserved for a year, with half the cores.
pid: 674ab89f-31ef-49ee-960a-f3e145dd5a5f
body: |2-
  Your table's c7i instances are EC2's compute-optimized line, the
  dearest kind. EC2's burstable ARM line (t4g) suits work that idles
  between builds, as this does; a 2-vCPU, 8 GB one with a disk and an
  address is about $59 a month, or $41 with a year's reservation.
  Hetzner's shared AMD machines cost half that and include an address
  and traffic; their US locations cost twice their European ones.
  Contabo is the price you once paid, with more memory, but its cheap
  vCPUs are known to be shared among many customers, so its speed is
  the least certain; a month's trial would say. Oracle's free ARM tier
  was halved this year and reclaims idle machines.
  ARM (t4g, Oracle) is untested for skg here; its native parts are C
  libraries that cargo compiles, so it should build.
  If you do not answer, nothing is rented.
contains:
- e2c0b9c1-7f3e-4871-92a2-f01b2d3f2525
